{"answer":"The critic’s central premise—that the skeptical grounded extension semantics is inherently non-monotone in the circuit-input sense—does not withstand scrutiny. The non-monotonicity the critic describes (accepting an argument, then removing an attacker, then the argument becomes unaccepted) is non-monotonicity of the *defeat relation*, not of the Boolean function the circuit computes over a fixed attack graph. Once the attack graph is fixed, the characteristic function that maps a candidate set S to the set of arguments that are not defeated by any argument outside S is monotone: enlarging S can only shrink the set of external defeaters, so the set of newly acceptable arguments can only grow. The grounded extension is then the least fixed point of this monotone operator, and its bit-wise indicator is a monotone Boolean function of the graph (equivalently, of the input bits encoding the candidate-acceptance vector at each iteration). A monotone circuit can therefore faithfully compute the least fixed point; the critic has not established any input bit whose 0→1 flip forces a later 1→0 output flip *under the fixed graph*, which is the only kind of non-monotonicity that would refute a monotone encoding. The reinstatement and cyclic-dependence phenomena are captured by iterating the monotone one-step operator to its least fixed point; they do not require non-monotone gates. Consequently the O(log n) depth and O(n^2) size are not shown to be artifacts of under-approximation. The critic’s second clause—that the size bound may fail for dense attack graphs—is a speculative caveat, not an established defect: for a graph with n nodes, the attack relation has at most n^2 entries, so an O(n^2)-size circuit is consistent with worst-case graph density. No fact is conceded beyond what the critic actually established, and the critic established neither inherent functional non-monotonicity nor a counterexample to the stated bounds."}